  "reveal": {
    "brief_voiced": "Xerxes Hartfield rang Spes, the second bell, and at the first change his rope fell silent. The green hand's trip at the tenth change—Wat Frew's stumble on Fides—convicts no one; a new ringer's fault is no fault at all. But Hartfield's silence at the outset admits no such excuse. His hands left the rope about the first minute of the touch, and in that absence the Colonel fell.\n\nThe Colonel's will lay unsettled, and Hartfield, a gentleman of great fortune, required no inheritance. Yet an unsettled will keeps others attentive—keeps them bound, keeps them waiting, keeps them subject to caprice. A man of few explanations and untroubled countenance has small patience for another man's fixed habits and choleric delays.\n\nThe rope tells what the ringer could not.",
